/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 4.5 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: Describe the construction, working, and calorific value calculation of a Bomb) Tj
T*
(Calorimeter with corrections.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Construction: The bomb calorimeter consists of a high-pressure,) Tj
T*
(corrosion-resistant stainless steel vessel \(bomb\) with a gas-tight screw lid, an inlet) Tj
T*
(valve for oxygen, and two electrical terminals connected to a platinum resistance fuse) Tj
T*
(wire. The fuel pellet \(~1 g\) sits in a silica crucible. The bomb is submerged in a) Tj
T*
(copper calorimeter containing weighed water \(W\) and an electrical stirrer, enclosed) Tj
T*
(within an air jacket and water jacket to prevent convective heat losses. Working: Oxygen) Tj
T*
(is filled to 25-30 atm. Fuel is ignited electrically. Water is continuously stirred, and) Tj
T*
(temperature is recorded using a Beckman thermometer readable to 0.001 deg C until peak) Tj
T*
(maximum is observed. Calculations: GCV = [ \(W + w\)*\(T2 - T1 + delta t\) - \(c_fuse +) Tj
T*
(c_acid + c_cotton\) ] / m. Corrections: \(1\) Cooling correction delta t: accounts for) Tj
T*
(radiation cooling; \(2\) Acid correction: exothermic formation of HNO3 and H2SO4) Tj
T*
(subtracted; \(3\) Fuse wire correction: heat released by burning fuse wire subtracted.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Differentiate between Proximate and Ultimate analysis of coal and explain their) Tj
T*
(industrial significance.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Proximate analysis determines empirical physical constituents \(Moisture,) Tj
T*
(Volatile Matter, Ash, Fixed Carbon\) using simple fractional heating regimes. Ultimate) Tj
T*
(analysis determines elemental chemical composition \(Carbon, Hydrogen, Nitrogen, Sulfur,) Tj
T*
(Oxygen\) via stoichiometry. Industrial Significance: \(1\) Moisture: Lowers calorific value) Tj
T*
(and consumes transport costs; preferred <2%. \(2\) Volatile Matter: High VM fuels ignite) Tj
T*
(readily and burn with a long flame, but generate excessive smoke and soot; low VM) Tj
T*
(preferred for metallurgical coke. \(3\) Ash: Non-combustible inorganic residue; high ash) Tj
T*
(reduces heating value, causes clinkering on boiler grates, and increases disposal costs.) Tj
T*
(\(4\) Fixed Carbon: Real heat-producing combustible core; higher FC corresponds directly) Tj
T*
(to superior calorific value and boiler thermal efficiency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
